it's been seven years since the release  of the last Star Ocean game but after  exceeding 30 hours buried in the  beautiful yet barren world of Star Ocean  integrity and faithlessness or Star  Ocean 5 it's difficult not to feel  disappointed its story and characters  are underdeveloped its mission design  fluctuates between uninteresting and  tedious and despite gratifyingly complex  combat and skill upgrade systems the  game is weighed down by a host of  frustrating smaller problem  I say I'm a perfectionist in Star Ocean  5 Fidel and his childhood friend Mickey  involved themselves in their home planet  Civil War and along the way discover a  bigger problem technologically advanced  beings from another world have arrived  and are using their home for nefarious  purposes along the way Fidel gathers a  ragtag band of not quite heroes to  rescue a mysterious little girl named  relia if we go with you then you may  never be able to return to this planet  again even if we do say Rio we might  never see our home again  unfortunately the narrative falls flat  characters are bland little to no  emotional development making it  difficult to care about their trials and  the plot does a poor job communicating  anyone's motivations there is a lot of  melodrama with no power behind it and  characters don't grow together and take  confusing emotional leaps the world of  Star Ocean 5 is big and beautiful but  feels just as empty as its characters  areas are vast and dungeons are  veritable mazes but none of it feels  alive enemy creatures spawn and clumps  in the same places every time making the  world predictable there's also no world  map and some areas are gated until you  progress far enough in the story making  the world feel small and defeating any  sense of exploration but everything is  better when you're in combat all  characters in your party fight at once  that you can only control one directly  at a time you can swap between  characters on the fly which comes in  handy during tougher boss battles where  you may want to oversee healing efforts  yourself battles are a flurry of motion  and light and are an absolute delight to  take part in even longer tougher boss  battles are welcome challenges and your  team dancing around the battlefield in  real time is a gorgeous display  that is if you properly develop and  assign combat roles and skills these are  unlocked by defeating enemies and  collecting special points which you  spend to upgrade roles and determine the  behavior of your characters in battle  you can unlock healing roles that  prioritize spells and assign them to one  character putting them in charge of  buffing the party and Dee buffing  enemies or you can give roles that  prioritize the use of special attacks to  your casters which ensures that keep out  of harm's way while still dealing the  highest amount of damage this creates  deep levels of customization within your  party and taking the time to unlock  develop and assign combat roles and  skills makes fighting a visual treat and  a breeze to navigate cooking up  assignments and strategies before major  boss battles is a highlight of my time  with Star Ocean 5  missions suffer from some odd issues too  many large-scale battles add much  unwelcome tower-defense and more  involved seemingly never-ending waves of  enemies with no direction for defeating  them still others pit you against  enemies you can deal no damage to until  dialogue leading up to the introduction  of a MacGuffin plays out these missions  are poorly designed and an abuse of your  time and resources side quests are  equally frustrating and very boring  although completing enough of them can  help you unlock specialty skills like  being able to see enemies on the map and  scavenging for resources but these  missions are nothing more than fetch  quests and not worth the effort it's  possible to never unlock certain  specialties because you never complete  enough or the right kind requests  Sargent 5 also suffers from a few weird  quirks characters constantly talk over  each other and too often while in a boss  battle or major conflict characters  would have conversations key to the plot  and I would be unable to hear them  pivotal conversations were lost to me  over the clash of swords and spells  I wish you the best sir  also Fidel can bump into and physically  move NPCs but we'll walk straight  through his teammates like their ghosts  it's incredibly jarring and pulls you  right out of the experience and Manuel  save points are infuriatingly few and  far between there are parts of the story  where the game actively blocks you from  saving between large battles and  unskipable cutscenesthat's is bloated  and empty with no worthwhile emotional  payoff and character development or  narrative combat and its subsequent  upgrade systems are genuinely fun but  the overall experience is held back from  being great by a lot of small things it  holds too tightly onto outdated  character tropes and concepts if you  came here searching for the epic sci-fi  glory of the series past this is not the  star ocean you are looking for  you
 

